# THE SIGNADOT PULL REQUEST: what the comment-drift review's fixes become.
#
# The second half of the program comment-drift.tony begins. The review delivers each fix as a
# branch under `verse/auto/<sha>/<task>`; this file turns the branches that can still land into
# ONE pull request against master, keeps that pull request current as master moves, and closes
# it when nothing is left to propose. Install it after comment-drift.tony:
#
#     verse charter -a -f docs/working/signadot/pr.tony
#
# ── WHY A PULL REQUEST, and what it changes ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# example/app's master is protected: a review is required, status checks are required, a
# merge queue sits in front, and nobody force-pushes or deletes it. So nothing verse runs can put
# a commit on master, and the merge gate verse-dev runs — a docket question whose yes IS the
# merge — has no act to release here. What a person decides is decided on the pull request, in
# the forge's own review, and the merge is the queue's.
#
# That moves the decision out of verse, and two things follow:
#
#   - NO GATE. verse-dev's gate is `!until [!quorum 1, !deadline "24h"]`, denying on the clock,
#     because a merge is the one act nobody can quietly undo. Pushing a branch and opening a pull
#     request undoes with a click, so the push runs `vector: auto` and asks nobody. The docket
#     stays empty by design; the pull request is where the question is.
#   - NO `decided`. verse-dev's train latches a per-tip decision so a denied batch is not asked
#     again. Here a declined pull request is a closed one, and it is not re-opened until master
#     moves and the review at the new tip proposes again — which is the same latch, held by the
#     forge rather than by a field.
#
# ── THE TRAIN IS STILL PER TIP, and the pull request outlives it ─────────────────
#
# One train per tip of master, as before, because a task's id is (rule, subject) and a spawn on
# an id that already completed returns the old answer: a singleton train would push once and
# never again. What is new is that every tip's train pushes the SAME ref, `verse/train`, forced,
# and the runner keeps one open pull request from that ref. So:
#
#   - master moves; the review at the new tip re-proposes whatever is still wrong (the ledger
#     hands it every open finding, and a fix that has not landed is still wrong); the new tip's
#     train is loaded with those fresh cars, rebuilt from the tip, and force-pushed. The open pull
#     request is UPDATED — that is what a push to its head is.
#   - the pull request merges; master moves; the review resolves what landed and re-proposes the
#     rest; if there is a rest, the next train's push opens a fresh pull request, since a merged
#     one is not open. If there is not, nothing is pushed and nothing is open.
#   - somebody closes the pull request unmerged; nothing here notices, deliberately: the branch
#     stands, the next tip's train force-pushes it, and the runner opens a new pull request. A
#     decline is a decision about THAT master.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE NEVER DOES IS CLOSE ONE. A pull request whose fixes became moot without merging
# — somebody corrected the comments by hand, or resolved the findings in the ledger — stays open
# until a person closes it, and the next master that the review proposes against overwrites it.
# Closing it would mean deleting `verse/train`, and the runner refuses a retiring destination
# over one fixed ref (config.go: a switch somebody pulls once by accident), which is the right
# rule and not one to make an exception to for tidiness.
#
# A train is rebuilt from the tip every time rather than rebased forward, because the review is
# idempotent by construction: anything the old train carried and master still lacks is proposed
# again at the new tip, so carrying the old cars forward would only put the same edit in twice.
# The cost is that a reviewer's comments on the forge go "outdated" when the branch is rebuilt
# under them, which is what a force-push to a pull request costs anywhere.
#
# THE STALENESS TEST IS UNCHANGED: a proposal records `base`, and `base != tip` retires it, with
# no ancestry walk and nothing asked. A car that does not rebase is skipped inside the task, and
# the charter does not learn which — git issue sxz4wejmh12kshy5g9n0 is that gap, and the pull
# request body is where it shows for now: the runner writes the train's address into it, and a
# branch listed on the train with no commit in the pull request is a car that was skipped.
#
# ── THE RUNNER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `verse.runners.pool.code` needs an environment named `signadot-pr`, the `verse-merge` shape
# pointed at `https://github.com/example/app`, checkout `signadot`, base `master`, with:
#
#   - `train`, ref `refs/heads/verse/train`, and ONE NEW OPTION: `pull: {base: master, title: …,
#     body: …}`. After the push lands, the runner makes sure an OPEN pull request from that ref
#     to the base exists, and opens one if none does — the pull-request half of the github
#     source already reads them; this is the one write. The token that pushes needs
#     `pull_requests: write` on the repository as well as `contents: write`.
#   - `retire`, ref `refs/heads/verse/auto/*/*`, `delete: true`, as verse-merge has.
#
# Both stand in deploy/coderunner/coderunner.yaml.
at: signadot.pr
reads: [git, signadot.comment-drift]
---
# A git ref IS a subject here: a delivered branch, and the tip.
- name: reflected
  level: 0
  match:
    system: git

- name: proposal
  level: 30
  match:
    path: [signadot, pr, proposal]

- name: train
  level: 40
  match:
    path: [signadot, pr, train]
---
# 1. A delivered branch becomes a proposal, and nothing else.
#
# `repo: signadot`, because the git source mirrors more than one repository and a ref named
# `verse/auto/…` in any of them reflects under the same kind. Without it this file would claim
# verse's deliveries too, and try to retire them through an environment that cannot reach them.
#
# TWO segments after `verse/auto/`, since `!glob` is filepath.Match and `*` does not cross `/`.
#
# FACTS ONLY: a derive restates its literal fields on every re-derive, so nothing here may be a
# state. `base` is pinned by the next rule, once.
- name: proposal-appeared
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      system: git
      kind: ref
      value:
        repo: signadot
        name: !glob "verse/auto/*/*"
  action:
    derive:
    # The branch is the id, so every reader below selects `[signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]` and
    # lands on one proposal rather than on the rule's subtree.
    - path: [signadot, pr, proposal]
      id: $subject.value.name
      value:
        repo: $subject.value.repo
        branch: $subject.value.name
        sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 1b. …and the commit it was cut from is pinned, once: writing `base` takes the proposal out of
# this rule's own selection.
- name: proposal-based
  stratum: proposal
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path base
  action:
    patch:
      base: !get-path {at: [git, head, signadot], path: sha}
---
# 1c. …and the proposal says what it is about: the review's report for the range that ends at
# its base, which is the review that proposed it unless master moved between the answer and the
# push landing.
- name: proposal-says
  stratum: proposal
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]
      value: !not.has-path state
  action:
    patch:
      says: !get-path
        at: [signadot, comment-drift, range, $subject.value.base]
        path: report
---
# 2. THE TRAIN: one per tip, so a new master is a new question. The ref every train pushes to
# is the environment's to say, so it is not restated here.
- name: train-cut
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git.head.signadot
  action:
    derive:
    - path: [signadot, pr, train]
      id: $subject.value.sha
      value:
        sha: $subject.value.sha
---
# 2b. …loaded with whatever is deliverable, and reloaded as that changes, while nothing has been
# pushed and only for the tip that is actually there.
#
# THE READS ARE IN THE CONDITION BECAUSE THAT IS WHAT WAKES IT. `reviewed` puts the review's
# report in the read set: the review pushes its branch and THEN answers, so the report always
# lands last, and a train that loaded on the proposal alone would load before the review had
# said anything — and the review at this tip is what settles which cars exist.
- name: train-load
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, pr, train, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path stage
      - !let
        let:
        - tip: !get-path {at: [git, head, signadot], path: sha}
        - fresh: !list-paths
            at: [signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]
            value: !not.has-path state
            path: branch
        - reviewed: !list-paths
            at: [signadot, comment-drift, range, "*"]
            value:
              status: done
            path: to
        in: !and
        - {sha: .[tip]}
        - {sha: !or .[reviewed]}
        - !not {branches: .[fresh]}
  action:
    patch:
      branches: !list-paths
        at: [signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]
        value: !not.has-path state
        path: branch
      says: !get-path
        at: [signadot, comment-drift, range, $subject.value.sha]
        path: report
---
# 2c. …and a loaded train is pushed, once, ungated. `stage` is the latch: the hooks write it and
# rule 2b stops reloading the moment it is there.
#
# THE CARS ARE FOUND IN THE CLONE, not interpolated: a charter has no list-to-text, and the task
# branch was cut from master at this tip, so what it fast-forwards is exactly a train rebuilt
# from the tip. Each car is rebased onto the task branch first — without that the first
# `--ff-only` moves the branch and every sibling is then neither ancestor nor descendant. A
# rebase that cannot apply is aborted and that car is skipped, which is safe because a comment
# fix is a textual edit localized to one symbol and there is no judgment for a conflict to need.
- name: train-push
  stratum: train
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, pr, train, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !has-path branches
      - !not {branches: []}
      - !not.has-path stage
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {at: [git, head, signadot], path: sha}}]
        in: {sha: .[tip]}
  action:
    patch:
      stage: pushing
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.code
      environment: signadot-pr
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      calls:
      - tool: run_bash
        input:
          command: "tb=$(git -C signadot rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD); for b in $(git -C signadot for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' 'refs/remotes/mirror/verse/auto/*/*'); do git -C signadot checkout -B _car \"$b\" || continue; if git -C signadot -c user.name='verse coderunner' -c user.email=coderunner@invalid rebase \"$tb\"; then git -C signadot checkout \"$tb\" && git -C signadot merge --ff-only _car; else git -C signadot rebase --abort || true; git -C signadot checkout \"$tb\"; fi; done"
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: train}
    onDone:
      patch:
        stage: pushed
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        err: $err
    # A cancel decided nothing, so the latch comes off and the train is pushed again.
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: !delete null
        err: !delete null
---
# 3. master moved, so the proposals cut from where it used to be can no longer land, and each
# one's branch is retired. Ungated: deleting a `verse/auto/*` branch destroys nothing that the
# next review does not remake, and a docket full of questions whose only answer is yes is a
# docket nobody reads.
- name: proposal-outrun
  stratum: proposal
  condition:
    subject:
      path: [signadot, pr, proposal, "*"]
      value: !and
      - !not.has-path state
      - !let
        let: [{tip: !get-path {at: [git, head, signadot], path: sha}}]
        in: {base: !not .[tip]}
  action:
    patch:
      state: stale
    spawn:
      runner: verse.runners.pool.code
      environment: signadot-pr
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      calls:
      - tool: push
        input: {destination: retire, ref: $branch}
    onFailed:
      patch:
        err: $err
